module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Blowup.Transform.FittingProduct
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Blowup.Transform.IntegralPerfectComplex
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.DivisorSum.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Perfect.GenericFreeCohomology

/-!
# Blowing up the product of all the Fitting ideals

Stacks tag 0ESU blows `X` up in *the product of the Fitting ideals* `Fit_k(ℱ)` over **all**
`k ≥ 0`, which makes sense because on an affine open the module of sections is generated by
finitely many elements, so `Fit_k(ℱ)` is the unit ideal once `k` exceeds that number: the family
is locally finite, only finitely many factors differing from `𝒪_X` near any point.

The centre here is the product over all `k ≥ 0`, and on the resulting blowup *every* Fitting
ideal of the pulled back module cuts out an effective Cartier divisor.

The construction is an intersection rather than an infinite product. On an affine open `U` the
truncated products `∏_{k ≤ N} Fit_k(ℱ)` decrease as `N` grows and are constant once `N` passes
the number of generators of `Γ(ℱ, U)`, so their intersection over all `N` is that stable value,
which is the honest product of the whole family. Intersecting makes sense for an arbitrary
family, so it can be written down first and identified with the stable truncation afterwards.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.fittingIdealProdDiff`: the product `∏_{k ≤ N, k ∉ e} Fit_k(ℱ)` of the
  Fitting ideal sheaves of index at most `N`, omitting the indices in a finite set `e`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.fittingIdealProdAllDiff`: the product of **all** the Fitting ideal sheaves
  of index not in `e`, as the intersection of those truncations.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.fittingIdealProdAll`: the case `e = ∅`, the centre of the blowup of tag
  0ESU.

## Main results

* `fittingIdeal_eq_top_of_le` and `exists_forall_fittingIdeal_eq_top`: the Fitting ideals of a
  finite module are the unit ideal in every index at least the number of generators.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_forall_fittingIdeal_ideal_eq_top`: the same on an affine open of a
  scheme, which is the local finiteness that makes the product over all `k` meaningful.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.fittingIdealProdAllDiff_ideal_eq_of_forall_eq_top`: on an affine open the
  product over all indices agrees with the truncation at any index past which the Fitting ideals
  are the unit ideal.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.isAdmissibleBlowup_blowUpToBase_fittingIdealProdAll`: the blowup of `X` in
  the product of all the Fitting ideals is a `U`-admissible blowup.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_fittingIdeal_pullback_fittingIdealProdAll`: on
  that blowup every Fitting ideal of the pulled back module is an effective Cartier divisor.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_isAdmissibleBlowup_isEffectiveCartierDivisor_fittingIdeal_pullback`:
  the two previous results combined, under the hypothesis `ℱ|_U = 0`.

## Implementation notes

**The omitted indices `e`.** The effective Cartier statement needs the centre factored as
`Fit_k(ℱ) · J` so that the summand lemma of tag 07ZV applies. The complementary factor `J` is the
product of all the Fitting ideals *except* the `k`-th, which is the same construction with one
index removed. Carrying a finite set `e` of omitted indices through the construction gives both
objects, and the stabilization argument, from one definition.

**Why `ofIdeals`.** The product over all indices is built with
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdeals`, the largest ideal sheaf below a family of
ideals, as the Fitting ideal sheaf itself is. That constructor needs no compatibility proof, so
the definition applies to an arbitrary sheaf of modules and the finiteness hypotheses enter only
when its sections are computed. Otherwise the hypotheses would appear in the *statement* of every
result about the blowup.

**The hypothesis `ℱ|_U = 0`.** The intermediate results are stated with finite local freeness of
rank `0`, `AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.IsFiniteLocallyFreeOfRank`, the form in which the
Fitting criterion of tag 0C3G applies. The final result is stated with
`CategoryTheory.Limits.IsZero`, which is the source's own hypothesis; the bridge is
`AlgebraicGeometry.isFiniteLocallyFreeOfRank_zero_of_isZero`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0ESU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ESU)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07Z7](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07Z7)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07ZA](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07ZA)
